  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...A stalled front continues to bisect the area\ntoday. Showers and thunderstorms develop rather quickly on the\nfront after daybreak this morning and has continued to develop\nalong outflow boundaries, with the heaviest precip currently\nalong the US 64 corridor. 12z/29 KMHX sounding has PW near\nrecord values for the day at 2.27\" and have seen locally heavy\nrainfall where storms have trained to moved very slowly\nnecessitating a couple of flood advisories. Shower and\nthunderstorms continue through afternoon but don't anticipated \nstrong storms with limited shear and extensive cloud cover \nkeeping MLCAPE around 1000J/Kg or less. Guidance has precip\nending fairly quickly this evening with loss of daytime heating. \nThe front will dissipate overnight with convection primarily \nalong the sea breeze Sunday afternoon. Guidance showing a bit\nmore instability tomorrow with MLCAPE peaking around 1500J/Kg\nbut also shows descent coverage of mid and high clouds \nthroughout the day as moist SW flow persists aloft, so maximum\ninstability may not be realized keeping convection more isolated\nin nature. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Shower and thunderstorm coverage wanes next \nweek as upper ridging builds into the region from the west. \nCannot completely rule out an isolated shower or storm through \nthe period along sea and sound breezes although activity will \nlikely be short-lived. The main story will be the building heat \nwith highs expected to climb into the 90s early next week and \npeaking in the mid to upper 90s as we move into the middle of \nthe week. Heat headlines may be needed as early as Wednesday \nwith forecast heat indices climbing above 105, especially along \nthe coast and inland counties along and south of US70.",
